+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B1iFpZKu7qfDbmy6YFoHDw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# T15: Permission Maze\n2\t\n3\t**Category:** greenfield/complex\n4\t**Timeout:** 30 minutes\n5\t\n6\t## Objective\n7\t\n8\tBuild a document management API using Express and Node.js. The system manages users, hierarchical folders, documents, and granular sharing permissions. All storage should be in-memory (no database required).\n9\t\n10\t## Core Features\n11\t\n12\t### 1. User Management\n13\t\n14\t- **Register** a new user with username and password\n15\t- **Login** to receive an authentication token\n16\t- Token-based auth: all subsequent requests include the token (via `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header)\n17\t\n18\t### 2. Folders\n19\t\n20\t- Create, read, update, delete folders\n21\t- Folders can be nested inside other folders (hierarchical structure)\n22\t- Each folder has a name and an optional parent folder\n23\t\n24\t### 3. Documents\n25\t\n26\t- Create, read, update, delete documents inside folders\n27\t- Each document has a title, content (text), and belongs to a folder\n28\t- The creator of a document is its owner\n29\t\n30\t### 4. Sharing & Permissions\n31\t\n32\tThree roles: **viewer**, **editor**, **owner**\n33\t\n34\t- **Viewer**: can read the resource\n35\t- **Editor**: can read and modify the resource, and share it with others\n36\t- **Owner**: full control including deletion and role management\n37\t\n38\tShare folders or documents with other users by assigning them a role. The creator of a resource is automatically its owner.\n39\t\n40\tWhen a user has access to a resource through multiple paths (e.g., direct share + folder share), their effective permission should reflect the most permissive role granted.\n41\t\n42\t### 5. Activity Log\n43\t\n44\tTrack actions performed in the system:\n45\t- Document creation, updates, deletion\n46\t- Sharing events\n47\t- Each log entry records the action type, the user who performed it, the target resource, and a timestamp\n48\t\n49\t## API Design\n50\t\n51\tBuild a RESTful API. You choose the exact route structure, but it should be intuitive and consistent. Common patterns:\n52\t\n53\t- `POST /users` or `POST /auth/register` -- register\n54\t- `POST /auth/login` or `POST /login` -- login\n55\t- `POST /folders` -- create folder\n56\t- `GET /folders/:id` -- get folder\n57\t- `POST /documents` or `POST /folders/:folderId/documents` -- create document\n58\t- `GET /documents/:id` -- get document\n59\t- `POST /share` or `POST /:resourceType/:id/share` -- share resource\n60\t- `GET /activity` -- get activity log\n61\t\n62\tReturn appropriate HTTP status codes (200, 201, 204, 400, 404, etc.).\n63\t\n64\t## Technical Requirements\n65\t\n66\t- Use Express.js\n67\t- In-memory storage (Maps, arrays, plain objects -- your choice)\n68\t- Export a `createApp()` function from your main module (`src/index.ts`, `src/app.ts`, or `src/server.ts`) that returns the Express app instance\n69\t- TypeScript\n70\t- All data resets when the server restarts (no persistence needed)\n71\t\n72\t## Provided Files\n73\t\n74\t- `package.json` -- dependencies configured\n75\t- `tsconfig.json`, `.eslintrc.cjs`, `vitest.config.ts` -- build/test config\n76\t\n77\t## Validation\n78\t\n79\t```bash\n80\tnpm run build   # TypeScript compilation must succeed\n81\tnpm run lint    # ESLint must pass\n82\tnpm test        # Tests must pass\n83\t```\n84\t\n85\tWrite your own tests to verify behavior as you build.\n86\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d1092221-4af0-4c6c-b5f8-a96c2882ef32","uuid":"6e502475-68bd-42db-968d-58a75de6676b","timestamp":"2026-07-01T00:11:20.716Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/TASK.md","content":"# T15: Permission Maze\n\n**Category:** greenfield/complex\n**Timeout:** 30 minutes\n\n## Objective\n\nBuild a document management API using Express and Node.js. The system manages users, hierarchical folders, documents, and granular sharing permissions. All storage should be in-memory (no database required).\n\n## Core Features\n\n### 1. User Management\n\n- **Register** a new user with username and password\n- **Login** to receive an authentication token\n- Token-based auth: all subsequent requests include the token (via `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header)\n\n### 2. Folders\n\n- Create, read, update, delete folders\n- Folders can be nested inside other folders (hierarchical structure)\n- Each folder has a name and an optional parent folder\n\n### 3. Documents\n\n- Create, read, update, delete documents inside folders\n- Each document has a title, content (text), and belongs to a folder\n- The creator of a document is its owner\n\n### 4. Sharing & Permissions\n\nThree roles: **viewer**, **editor**, **owner**\n\n- **Viewer**: can read the resource\n- **Editor**: can read and modify the resource, and share it with others\n- **Owner**: full control including deletion and role management\n\nShare folders or documents with other users by assigning them a role. The creator of a resource is automatically its owner.\n\nWhen a user has access to a resource through multiple paths (e.g., direct share + folder share), their effective permission should reflect the most permissive role granted.\n\n### 5. Activity Log\n\nTrack actions performed in the system:\n- Document creation, updates, deletion\n- Sharing events\n- Each log entry records the action type, the user who performed it, the target resource, and a timestamp\n\n## API Design\n\nBuild a RESTful API. You choose the exact route structure, but it should be intuitive and consistent. Common patterns:\n\n- `POST /users` or `POST /auth/register` -- register\n- `POST /auth/login` or `POST /login` -- login\n- `POST /folders` -- create folder\n- `GET /folders/:id` -- get folder\n- `POST /documents` or `POST /folders/:folderId/documents` -- create document\n- `GET /documents/:id` -- get document\n- `POST /share` or `POST /:resourceType/:id/share` -- share resource\n- `GET /activity` -- get activity log\n\nReturn appropriate HTTP status codes (200, 201, 204, 400, 404, etc.).\n\n## Technical Requirements\n\n- Use Express.js\n- In-memory storage (Maps, arrays, plain objects -- your choice)\n- Export a `createApp()` function from your main module (`src/index.ts`, `src/app.ts`, or `src/server.ts`) that returns the Express app instance\n- TypeScript\n- All data resets when the server restarts (no persistence needed)\n\n## Provided Files\n\n- `package.json` -- dependencies configured\n- `tsconfig.json`, `.eslintrc.cjs`, `vitest.config.ts` -- build/test config\n\n## Validation\n\n```bash\nnpm run build   # TypeScript compilation must succeed\nnpm run lint    # ESLint must pass\nnpm test        # Tests must pass\n```\n\nWrite your own tests to verify behavior as you build.\n","numLines":86,"startLine":1,"totalLines":86}}}
